  "text": "No restart needed -- hooks are loaded fresh each time they fire, so the `chmod +x` fix is already active. But here's how to verify and debug:\n\n**1. Check the Hooks output channel** -- this is the best way to see if hooks are running:\n- Open the Command Palette: `Cmd+Shift+P`\n- Type \"Output: Show Output Channel\"\n- Select **\"Hooks\"** from the dropdown\n\nThis shows exactly what Cursor tried to run, what it received back, and any errors.\n\n**2. If you want to be safe, reload the window** (not a full restart):\n- `Cmd+Shift+P` → **\"Developer: Reload Window\"**\n\n**3. Important caveat about `beforeSubmitPrompt`** -- According to the [Cursor hooks deep dive](https://blog.gitbutler.com/cursor-hooks-deep-dive) by Scott Chacon, the `beforeSubmitPrompt` hook was **informational only** in the beta -- Cursor did not respect the `continue: false` output to actually block prompts. Only `beforeShellExecution`, `beforeMCPExecution`, and `beforeReadFile` hooks supported blocking via the `continue`/`permission` response fields.\n\nThis may have changed in newer Cursor versions, but if your hook still doesn't block after the permission fix, that's likely why. In that case, the hook can only *log* the violation, not *prevent* it. You'd want to check the Hooks output channel to see what's happening.",
