  "text": "Here's a complete guide for your demo, including what tests are, how to ask Cursor to find gaps, and how to ask it to generate tests.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Primer: What Are Test Cases?\n\nTests are small programs that verify your actual code works correctly. For example, if you have a `BookmarksPage` component that shows bookmarks or an empty state, a test would:\n\n1. Render the component with fake data\n2. Check that the right things appear on screen (e.g., \"Are bookmark cards showing?\" or \"Is the empty message showing when there are no bookmarks?\")\n\nIn this repo, **frontend tests** use **Jest** (the test runner) and **React Testing Library** (to render components and simulate user clicks). Test files live next to the code they test and are named `*.test.tsx`.\n\n---\n\n## Demo Part 1: Ask Cursor to Scan for Test Coverage Gaps\n\nHere are example prompts you could use, going from broad to narrow:\n\n### Broad scan (good for showing the \"discovery\" capability):\n\n> **\"Scan `public/app/features/bookmarks/` and `public/app/features/playlist/` for components that don't have corresponding test files. Summarize what each untested component does and rank them by how much they'd benefit from tests.\"**\n\n### Targeted scan (good for a focused demo):\n\n> **\"Look at `public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.tsx` -- it has no tests. Analyze the component and tell me what test cases would be valuable. List specific scenarios that should be tested.\"**\n\nCursor would respond with something like:\n\n- **Empty state** -- when there are no pinned/bookmarked items, the empty message should render\n- **Populated state** -- when there are pinned items, `NavLandingPageCard` cards should render for each one\n- **Filtering invalid items** -- when `findByUrl` returns `undefined` for some URLs, those should be excluded\n- **Card content** -- each card should display the correct `text`, `description`, and `url`\n\n### Another good scan prompt for a larger area:\n\n> **\"In `public/app/features/playlist/`, some files have tests and some don't. Compare the tested vs untested files. Which untested files have the most complex logic and would benefit most from test coverage?\"**\n\n---\n\n## Demo Part 2: Ask Cursor to Generate the Tests\n\nOnce you've identified the gap, switch to **Agent mode** and ask Cursor to write the tests. Here are example prompts:\n\n### Simple and direct:\n\n> **\"Write tests for `public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.tsx`. Follow the same testing patterns used in `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistForm.test.tsx` as a reference.\"**\n\n### More guided (gives better results):\n\n> **\"Create a test file `BookmarksPage.test.tsx` for the `BookmarksPage` component in `public/app/features/bookmarks/`. The tests should:\n> 1. Mock `usePinnedItems` to return different sets of URLs\n> 2. Mock the Redux nav tree with `useSelector`\n> 3. Test the empty state when no bookmarks exist\n> 4. Test that bookmark cards render correctly when items are pinned\n> 5. Test that invalid/missing URLs are filtered out\n>\n> Use the same testing patterns as other tests in this repo (React Testing Library, jest.mock, describe/it blocks).\"**\n\n### For a hook or utility (non-visual logic):\n\n> **\"Write unit tests for the `usePlaylistItems` hook in `public/app/features/playlist/usePlaylistItems.tsx`. Test adding, removing, and reordering items. Follow the testing patterns in this repo.\"**\n\n---\n\n## What the Generated Test Would Look Like\n\nFor context, here's the pattern Cursor would follow. An existing test in this repo like `PlaylistForm.test.tsx` looks like this:\n\n```1:12:public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistForm.test.tsx\nimport { render, screen, within } from '@testing-library/react';\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';\n\nimport { Playlist } from '../../api/clients/playlist/v0alpha1';\n\nimport { PlaylistForm } from './PlaylistForm';\n\njest.mock('app/core/components/TagFilter/TagFilter', () => ({\n  TagFilter: () => {\n    return <>mocked-tag-filter</>;\n  },\n}));\n```\n\nA generated `BookmarksPage.test.tsx` would follow that same structure -- imports from `@testing-library/react`, `jest.mock()` calls for dependencies like `usePinnedItems`, mock data for the nav tree, and `describe`/`it` blocks checking what renders.\n\n---\n\n## Best Demo Flow (Step by Step)\n\nHere's a suggested script for your demo:\n\n**Step 1 -- Discovery (Ask mode).** Paste this prompt:\n\n> \"Analyze `public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.tsx`. It has no test file. What test scenarios should I write for this component? Be specific about edge cases.\"\n\nThis shows Cursor understanding the code and identifying testable behaviors.\n\n**Step 2 -- Generation (switch to Agent mode).** Paste this prompt:\n\n> \"Generate a complete test file for `BookmarksPage.tsx` at `public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.test.tsx`. Cover the scenarios you identified: empty state, populated state, and filtering invalid items. Follow the existing test patterns in `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistForm.test.tsx`.\"\n\nThis shows Cursor writing a real, runnable test file.\n\n**Step 3 -- Run the tests (still Agent mode).** Ask:\n\n> \"Run the tests you just created with `npx jest public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.test.tsx` and fix any failures.\"\n\nThis shows the iterative loop -- Cursor generates, runs, and self-corrects.\n\n---\n\n## Other Good Areas for a Demo\n\nIf `BookmarksPage` feels too simple, here are progressively more complex options:\n\n| Component | Why it's good for demo |\n|---|---|\n| `BookmarksPage.tsx` (bookmarks) | Simple, zero tests, easy to understand |\n| `StartModal.tsx` (playlist) | Medium complexity, feature flags, multiple states |\n| `usePlaylistItems.tsx` (playlist) | Hook with add/remove/reorder logic, shows non-UI testing |\n| `DeleteDashboardButton.tsx` (dashboard settings) | Complex flow with delete confirmation, provisioning checks |\n| Entire `alerting/unified/triage/` directory | ~19 components with zero tests, shows large-scale scan |\n\nThe bookmarks example is ideal for a demo because the component is small (67 lines), the logic is clear, and the audience can follow along without needing deep Grafana knowledge.",
