  "text": "Here's a breakdown of this file in plain terms:\n\n---\n\n### Imports (lines 1-10)\n\nThese pull in tools and components the page needs:\n\n- **`css`** from Emotion -- a CSS-in-JS library that lets you write CSS styles as JavaScript objects.\n- **`GrafanaTheme2`, `NavModelItem`** -- TypeScript types. `GrafanaTheme2` describes the shape of the Grafana theme (colors, spacing, etc.). `NavModelItem` describes a navigation menu entry.\n- **`Trans`, `t`** -- internationalization (i18n) helpers. `t()` translates a string, `<Trans>` translates a block of JSX.\n- **`EmptyState`, `useStyles2`** -- UI components/hooks from Grafana's component library. `EmptyState` shows a placeholder when there's no data. `useStyles2` connects a style-generating function to the current theme.\n- **`usePinnedItems`** -- a custom hook that returns the list of URLs the user has bookmarked/pinned.\n- **`findByUrl`** -- a utility that looks up a navigation item by its URL in the nav tree.\n- **`NavLandingPageCard`** -- a card component used to display a navigation item visually.\n- **`Page`** -- the standard Grafana page wrapper (provides layout, header, scrolling, etc.).\n- **`useSelector`** -- a Redux hook that reads data from the global Redux store.\n\n---\n\n### The Component (lines 12-53)\n\n```12:53:public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.tsx\nexport function BookmarksPage() {\n  const styles = useStyles2(getStyles);\n  const pinnedItems = usePinnedItems();\n  const navTree = useSelector((state) => state.navBarTree);\n\n  const validItems = pinnedItems.reduce((acc: NavModelItem[], url) => {\n    const item = findByUrl(navTree, url);\n    if (item) {\n      acc.push(item);\n    }\n    return acc;\n  }, []);\n  // ... rendering ...\n}\n```\n\nStep by step:\n\n1. **`const styles = useStyles2(getStyles)`** -- Calls the `getStyles` function (defined at the bottom) with the current Grafana theme, producing a `styles` object with CSS class names you can apply to elements.\n\n2. **`const pinnedItems = usePinnedItems()`** -- Gets the list of URLs the user has bookmarked. This is an array of strings like `[\"/dashboards\", \"/alerting\"]`.\n\n3. **`const navTree = useSelector((state) => state.navBarTree)`** -- Reads the full navigation tree from the Redux store. This is Grafana's hierarchical menu data (all the sidebar items).\n\n4. **`const validItems = pinnedItems.reduce(...)`** -- This is the key data transformation. It loops through each pinned URL and tries to find its matching navigation item in the nav tree:\n   - Start with an empty array (`[]`).\n   - For each `url`, call `findByUrl(navTree, url)` to look it up.\n   - If a matching `item` is found, push it into the accumulator array.\n   - Skip any URLs that don't match a current nav item (e.g., if a bookmarked page was removed).\n   - The result is an array of `NavModelItem` objects -- only the bookmarks that still exist in the nav.\n\n---\n\n### The JSX / Rendering (lines 25-52)\n\nThe return statement renders the page:\n\n- **`<Page navId=\"bookmarks\">`** -- Wraps everything in Grafana's standard page layout, associating it with the \"bookmarks\" navigation entry (so the sidebar highlights correctly).\n\n- **`<Page.Contents>`** -- The main content area of the page.\n\n- **Conditional rendering (`validItems.length === 0 ? ... : ...`):**\n  - **If no bookmarks exist:** Show an `<EmptyState>` with a translated message telling the user they haven't created any bookmarks yet, plus a tip on how to add them.\n  - **If bookmarks exist:** Render a CSS grid (`<section className={styles.grid}>`) and map over each `validItem`, rendering a `<NavLandingPageCard>` for each one. Each card shows the item's name (`text`), description (`subTitle`), and links to its URL.\n\n- **`key={item.id || item.url}`** -- React requires a unique `key` for each item in a list. It prefers the item's `id`, falling back to `url`.\n\n- **`url={item.url ?? ''}`** -- The nullish coalescing operator (`??`). If `item.url` is `null` or `undefined`, use an empty string instead.\n\n---\n\n### The Styles (lines 56-64)\n\n```56:64:public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.tsx\nconst getStyles = (theme: GrafanaTheme2) => ({\n  grid: css({\n    display: 'grid',\n    gap: theme.spacing(3),\n    gridTemplateColumns: 'repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr))',\n    gridAutoRows: '138px',\n    padding: theme.spacing(2, 0),\n  }),\n});\n```\n\nThis is a function that receives the current theme and returns an object of CSS classes:\n\n- **`display: 'grid'`** -- Uses CSS Grid layout.\n- **`gap: theme.spacing(3)`** -- Spacing between grid items, pulled from the theme (so it's consistent across the app).\n- **`gridTemplateColumns: 'repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr))'`** -- Creates as many columns as will fit, each at least 300px wide. This makes the grid responsive -- fewer columns on narrow screens, more on wide ones.\n- **`gridAutoRows: '138px'`** -- Each row is exactly 138px tall.\n- **`padding: theme.spacing(2, 0)`** -- Vertical padding (top/bottom) with no horizontal padding.\n\n---\n\n### The Export (line 66)\n\n```66:66:public/app/features/bookmarks/BookmarksPage.tsx\nexport default BookmarksPage;\n```\n\nThe component is exported both as a **named export** (line 12: `export function BookmarksPage`) and as a **default export** (line 66). This is a common pattern in Grafana -- it allows other files to import it either way: `import BookmarksPage` or `import { BookmarksPage }`.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis page displays the user's bookmarked navigation items as a responsive grid of cards. It fetches the pinned URLs, resolves them against the current navigation tree to get their metadata (title, description), filters out any that no longer exist, and either shows the grid of cards or an empty state message encouraging the user to add bookmarks.",
