  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Now I have a clear picture. Here's how I'd structure this demo.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Demo Workflow\n\n### The Setup\n\n**Notion page** (you'd create this once): A short \"Test Requirements\" doc for the `ShareMenu` component. This is the ideal target because:\n\n- The component is only 141 lines (`public/app/features/dashboard-scene/sharing/ShareButton/ShareMenu.tsx`)\n- It already has a test file with clear patterns to follow (`ShareMenu.test.tsx`, 100 lines)\n- There are obvious gaps in the existing tests that make for natural requirements\n\n**Example Notion doc content** (2-3 requirements keeps it fast):\n\n```markdown\n# ShareMenu Test Requirements\n\n## TC-001: Custom share drawer items render\nWhen a custom share drawer item is registered via `addDashboardShareDrawerItem`,\nit should appear in the rendered menu alongside the built-in items.\n\n## TC-002: Dirty dashboard triggers save modal\nWhen the dashboard is in editing mode and has unsaved changes (`isEditing: true, isDirty: true`),\nclicking a share menu item should show the `SaveBeforeShareModal` instead of immediately executing the share action.\n\n## TC-003: Cleanup resets custom items\nAfter calling `resetDashboardShareDrawerItems()`, previously registered custom items\nshould no longer appear in the menu.\n```\n\n### The Agent Flow (3 steps)\n\n**Step 1 — Search Notion for the requirements doc**\n\n```typescript\n// Agent calls: notion-search\n{ \"query\": \"ShareMenu test requirements\", \"query_type\": \"internal\", \"page_size\": 3 }\n```\n\nThis returns a page ID/URL from the search results.\n\n**Step 2 — Fetch the full doc**\n\n```typescript\n// Agent calls: notion-fetch\n{ \"id\": \"<page-id-from-search>\" }\n```\n\nThis returns the structured test requirements in Markdown.\n\n**Step 3 — Agent writes tests**\n\nThe agent reads the existing `ShareMenu.test.tsx` to understand patterns (imports, `setup()` helper, `jest.mock` usage), then adds the new test cases following the same conventions.\n\n### Why This Scope Works for a Quick Demo\n\n| Factor | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Notion calls** | 2 total (search + fetch) |\n| **Files to read** | 2 (`ShareMenu.tsx` + `ShareMenu.test.tsx`) |\n| **Files to edit** | 1 (`ShareMenu.test.tsx`) |\n| **Tests to add** | 2-3 small test cases |\n| **Complexity** | Low — the existing test patterns are copy-paste friendly |\n\nThe existing test file already imports everything needed (`render`, `screen`, `DashboardScene`, `contextSrv`, etc.), so the agent doesn't need to figure out any new dependencies. And the `setup()` helper accepts `Partial<DashboardSceneState>`, making it trivial to test different state combinations.\n\n### What the New Tests Would Look Like\n\nFor reference, TC-002 (dirty dashboard save modal) would be something like:\n\n```typescript\nit('should show save modal when dashboard is dirty', async () => {\n  const user = userEvent.setup();\n  setup({ isEditing: true, isDirty: true, meta: { canEdit: true } });\n\n  const shareInternally = await screen.findByTestId(selector.shareInternally);\n  await user.click(shareInternally);\n\n  expect(await screen.findByText(/save before sharing/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\n});\n```\n\nThat's a simple, self-contained test that follows the existing patterns exactly.\n\n### Prompt for the Demo\n\nWhen you're ready to run it, a prompt like this would kick things off:\n\n> \"Search Notion for 'ShareMenu test requirements', pull the test cases from that doc, then implement them in `public/app/features/dashboard-scene/sharing/ShareButton/ShareMenu.test.tsx` following the existing test patterns.\"\n\n### Alternative: Even Tighter Scope\n\nIf you want to cut it down to the absolute minimum, you could:\n- Put just **one** test requirement in Notion (TC-002 is the most visually interesting since it tests user interaction)\n- That reduces it to: 2 MCP calls, read 2 files, add ~10 lines to one file\n\nWould you like me to dig into any part of this in more detail, like the exact Notion doc structure or how to set up the feature flag gating for a more realistic scenario?",
