  "text": "Here's the concrete demo plan, grounded in this Grafana codebase (53k+ source files, 777k+ lines, Go backend + TypeScript frontend, 36 top-level dirs).\n\n---\n\n## Demo Plan: Navigating Large Complex Codebases in Cursor\n\n### Step 1: Scope the workspace down\n\n**Problem:** Cursor indexes everything. In a 53k-file monorepo, that's noise.\n\n**Demo moves:**\n- **`.cursorignore`** — Exclude generated code, vendor dirs, build artifacts, changelogs. This repo has none; show creating one:\n\n```\n.yarn/\nnode_modules/\nvendor/\n**/wire_gen.go\n**/*_gen.go\n**/mocks/\ne2e-playwright/\n.changelog-archive/\n```\n\n- **Open subfolders as workspaces** — If you only care about the frontend dashboard feature, open `public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` as its own workspace. Indexing goes from 53k files to ~200. Show the difference in semantic search quality.\n\n### Step 2: Encode institutional knowledge in Rules\n\n**Problem:** Legacy codebases have patterns, conventions, and gotchas that no AI can infer from code alone.\n\n**Demo moves:**\n- Show the existing `AGENTS.md` (already in this repo with docs conventions)\n- Show the existing `.cursor/rules/grafana-build-patterns.mdc` rule (already loaded here) — it encodes wire DI patterns, variable shadowing traps, `@ts-expect-error` conventions for experimental props, and the `Field` single-child gotcha\n- Create a new rule live: pick a pattern from the codebase (e.g., \"all new dashboard settings pages follow this 3-step registration pattern\") and encode it as a `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` file\n- Key point: **Rules are how you teach Cursor the things README files never captured**\n\n### Step 3: Use `@`-mentions for surgical context\n\n**Problem:** The agent can't read 53k files. You must point it.\n\n**Demo moves:**\n- `@public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` — scope a question to one feature module\n- `@pkg/services/dashboards/` — scope to the backend counterpart\n- `@Makefile` — give it build context\n- Show asking \"How does dashboard versioning work?\" with vs. without `@`-mentions — dramatic quality difference\n- Tag 2-3 specific files for a cross-cutting question (e.g., `@transformSceneToSaveModel.ts` + `@transformSaveModelToScene.ts` for serialization round-trip)\n\n### Step 4: Explorer sub-agents for unknown territory\n\n**Problem:** You don't always know which files to tag. Legacy codebases are full of surprises.\n\n**Demo moves:**\n- Launch an `explore` sub-agent: *\"How does the alerting feature handle notification routing? Be very thorough.\"* with `target_directories: [\"pkg/services/alerting/\"]`\n- Launch a parallel `explore` agent for the frontend side: *\"How does the alerting UI render notification policies?\"* with `target_directories: [\"public/app/features/alerting/\"]`\n- Show that both run concurrently and return structured findings\n- Key point: **Sub-agents are disposable investigators — use them to map unknown territory before you start editing**\n\n### Step 5: Semantic Search vs. Grep — know when to use which\n\n**Problem:** Legacy codebases have inconsistent naming. `grep` misses conceptual matches.\n\n**Demo moves:**\n- **Grep** for exact symbols: `DashboardScene`, `RegisterAPIService`, `wire.NewSet` — fast, precise\n- **Semantic Search** for concepts: *\"Where is permission checked before saving a dashboard?\"* — finds code even if it's called `guardianBase.CheckSave` not `checkPermission`\n- Show a side-by-side: grep for \"permission\" returns 500+ results; semantic search for \"where are dashboard save permissions enforced?\" returns the 3 files that matter\n- Key point: **Grep for what you know the name of; Semantic Search for what you know the concept of**\n\n### Step 6: Demonstrate a real task end-to-end\n\n**Problem:** The previous steps are tools. Show them working together.\n\n**Demo task:** *\"Add a new field to dashboard settings\"* (a realistic cross-cutting change)\n\n1. **Explore** — sub-agent maps the existing settings pattern\n2. **Rules fire** — the `grafana-build-patterns.mdc` rule tells the agent the exact 4-step pattern (interface → PERSISTED_PROPS → serialize → deserialize)\n3. **`@`-mentions** — tag the 3 specific files that need changes\n4. **Implementation** — agent makes the changes following the encoded pattern\n5. **Verify** — linter check, type check\n\nThis shows the full loop: scope → context → knowledge → execution.\n\n---\n\n### Talking Points Summary\n\n| Challenge | Cursor Strategy |\n|---|---|\n| Too many files indexed | `.cursorignore`, open subfolders |\n| Undocumented conventions | `.cursor/rules/`, `AGENTS.md` |\n| Agent lacks context | `@`-mention specific files/folders |\n| Don't know where to look | Explorer sub-agents (parallel) |\n| Inconsistent naming | Semantic Search for concepts, Grep for symbols |\n| Cross-cutting changes | Combine all of the above in sequence |\n\nThe throughline: **in a legacy codebase, the human's job shifts from writing code to curating context.** Rules, scoping, and targeted `@`-mentions are how you make the agent effective in a codebase that wasn't built for AI tooling.",
