  "text": "Honest answer: **opening a subfolder wouldn't be realistic here, and an engineer in the audience would spot it.**\n\nHere's why, and what to do instead:\n\n### Why subfolders don't work for Grafana\n\nEven the most self-contained feature module, `public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` (545 files), immediately imports from outside itself:\n\n```\nimport { CoreApp, DataQueryRequest, locationUtil, ... } from '@grafana/data';\nimport { config, locationService, RefreshEvent } from '@grafana/runtime';\n```\n\nAnd `packages/grafana-ui` (844 files) has a tsconfig that literally reaches into `../../public/app/types/`. If you opened either as a standalone workspace:\n\n- TypeScript resolution breaks (no `@grafana/*` paths)\n- You can't lint, can't run tests, can't build\n- Cursor's LSP-based features (go-to-definition, find references) go dead\n\nAn engineer would never do this with Grafana. They'd open the full repo. The subfolder-as-workspace pattern only makes sense for truly independent packages (think a pnpm monorepo where each package has its own `node_modules` and builds independently).\n\n### What's actually realistic (and more impressive to show)\n\n**Open the full 53k-file repo and show how you tame it.** That's the real skill, and it's more compelling because it mirrors what engineers actually face.\n\nThe demo flow that would resonate:\n\n1. **Open the full repo** — acknowledge the scale (\"53k source files, mixed Go and TypeScript\")\n2. **`.cursorignore`** — immediately cut the noise (generated files, vendor, changelogs, e2e). You're not shrinking the workspace physically, you're telling Cursor what not to index.\n3. **Scope with `@`-mentions** — \"I own the dashboard-scene feature. I don't need to read 53k files, I need these 545.\" Tag `@public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` and ask a question. Show how the quality of answers jumps.\n4. **Rules for tribal knowledge** — show the build-patterns rule already in this repo. Point out how it encodes things like \"the `Field` component only accepts a single child\" — the kind of bug that wastes 30 minutes without a rule, zero minutes with one.\n5. **Make a scoped change** — something like adding a new setting to the dashboard settings page. The agent follows the encoded pattern, touches only the 3-4 files it needs, and you never leave the full workspace.\n\n### The one-liner for the audience\n\n> \"You don't shrink the codebase to fit the AI. You teach the AI where to look and what matters.\"\n\nThat's the real pattern. `.cursorignore` for what to ignore, `@`-mentions for what to focus on, rules for what to know. It's more honest, more practical, and engineers will recognize it as their actual workflow.",
