  "text": "Good question. Looking at what already exists in General Settings (title, description, tags, folder, editable, time settings, tooltip, preload), here are three options ranked by demo suitability:\n\n### Best for demo: **Auto-collapse rows on load**\n\nA simple boolean toggle: \"Collapse all rows when the dashboard first loads.\" \n\nWhy it's great for a demo:\n- **Realistic** — this is a real feature request from Grafana users (dashboards with 20+ rows are overwhelming on load)\n- **Small surface area** — it's a single boolean, so you can implement it live in ~10 minutes\n- **Hits the multi-file pattern** documented in your rules file — you'll need to touch exactly these files:\n  1. `DashboardScene.tsx` — add `autoCollapseRows?: boolean` to `DashboardSceneState`, add to `PERSISTED_PROPS`\n  2. `transformSceneToSaveModel.ts` — serialize it (with the `@ts-expect-error` experimental pattern)\n  3. `transformSaveModelToScene.ts` — deserialize it\n  4. `GeneralSettingsEditView.tsx` — add a `Switch` toggle in the Panel Options section (right next to \"Preload panels\", which is the same pattern)\n\nThat's 4 files, all in the `dashboard-scene/` feature area, all following the exact pattern your rules already encode. It's the cleanest possible demo of \"rules + `@`-mentions + scoped change.\"\n\n### Runners-up\n\n- **Dashboard accent color** (a color picker) — more visual, but the color picker component adds UI complexity that could eat demo time\n- **Custom \"no panels\" empty state message** — too niche, audience might not connect with it\n\n### Why this would feel authentic to engineers\n\nAn engineer watching this demo would see:\n\n1. You open the full Grafana repo (not a toy project)\n2. You `@`-mention `@public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` and ask Cursor how dashboard settings work\n3. The rules fire and tell the agent the exact 4-file pattern\n4. The agent makes the change across 4 files, following the established conventions (including `@ts-expect-error` for experimental props, `PERSISTED_PROPS` for undo/redo)\n5. You run the type checker to verify\n\nNo engineer would question that workflow — it's exactly how someone on the Grafana dashboards team would work. The only thing you're skipping is running the UI, which is fine because the point is the navigation and context management, not the runtime.",
