  "text": "The exploration just surfaced the perfect rule candidate. Here's what's worth encoding and why:\n\n### The rule: **\"Dashboard Property Lifecycle\"**\n\nThe single most valuable thing you learned from that exploration is that adding any new property to `DashboardScene` requires touching a **non-obvious chain of 6 concerns**, not just 4. An engineer who doesn't know this will get a property that \"works\" in the UI but silently breaks dirty detection, save, or load:\n\n| Step | File(s) | What goes wrong if you skip it |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Add to `DashboardSceneState` interface | `scene/DashboardScene.tsx` | Won't compile |\n| 2. Add to `PERSISTED_PROPS` | `scene/DashboardScene.tsx` | Undo/redo ignores it, dirty detection misses it |\n| 3. Serialize in v1 | `serialization/transformSceneToSaveModel.ts` | Doesn't save (v1 API path) |\n| 4. Serialize in v2 | `serialization/transformSceneToSaveModelSchemaV2.ts` | Doesn't save (v2 API path) |\n| 5. Deserialize in v1 | `serialization/transformSaveModelToScene.ts` | Loads as undefined |\n| 6. Deserialize in v2 | `serialization/transformSaveModelSchemaV2ToScene.ts` | Loads as undefined (v2 path) |\n\nThe gotcha that makes this rule valuable: **most engineers would only do steps 1, 3, and 5** — they'd miss `PERSISTED_PROPS`, and they'd miss the v2 serialization stack entirely. The property would appear to work during development, then fail in production when the v2 API path is active or when a user tries to undo.\n\n### What the demo flow looks like\n\n1. **Explore** — you ask Cursor to explore `@public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` and understand the architecture\n2. **Learn the non-obvious** — you see the dual serialization stacks, `PERSISTED_PROPS`, change tracker\n3. **Encode it** — you create a rule right there in the session:\n\n```\n@public/app/features/dashboard-scene/ create a cursor rule that documents how to add a new property to DashboardScene, including all files that must be updated and in what order.\n```\n\n4. **Use it** — now when you prompt \"add auto-collapse rows on load,\" the rule fires and the agent hits all 6 steps automatically\n\n### The narrative for the audience\n\n> \"I just spent 2 minutes exploring a folder I'd never seen before. The AI found a 6-step lifecycle that would have taken me an hour of trial-and-error. Now I've encoded that into a rule, and every future change — by me or anyone on my team — gets it right the first time. The rule is version-controlled alongside the code.\"\n\nThat's the story: **exploration → institutional knowledge → encoded rule → compounding returns.** It's not just about navigating the codebase today — it's about making every future session faster.",
