  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Here's a prioritized list of migration candidates, grouped by complexity and risk.\n\n---\n\n### Tier 1: Trivial import fixes (change import source only, zero behavior change)\n\nThese 4 files import `useLocation` from the wrong package. The fix is a single-line import change to `react-router-dom-v5-compat`:\n\n| File | Current Import | Change |\n|------|---------------|--------|\n| `public/app/core/components/AppNotifications/AppNotificationList.tsx` | `useLocation` from `react-router-dom` | Switch to `react-router-dom-v5-compat` |\n| `public/app/features/provisioning/Repository/RepositoryStatusPage.tsx` | `useLocation` from `react-router` | Switch to `react-router-dom-v5-compat` |\n| `public/app/features/provisioning/File/FileStatusPage.tsx` | `useLocation` from `react-router` | Switch to `react-router-dom-v5-compat` |\n| `public/app/features/dashboard-scene/scene/layout-tabs/TabItemRenderer.tsx` | `useLocation` from `react-router` | Switch to `react-router-dom-v5-compat` |\n\nThese are safe because the v5 `useLocation` and the compat `useLocation` return the same shape from the same router context. They just unify the import source.\n\n---\n\n### Tier 2: Test helper modernization (medium scope, test-only risk)\n\nThese files use v5 `<Router history={}>` and `<Route render={}>` directly. Updating them exercises the compat patterns in test infrastructure without touching production routing:\n\n| File | What's v5 | Migration scope |\n|------|-----------|----------------|\n| `public/app/features/explore/spec/helper/setup.tsx` | `<Router history={history}>` + `<Route path=\"...\" render={props => ...}>` (lines 201-225) | Replace `Route render` with `Route element`, wrap in `CompatRouter`. ~20 lines changed. |\n| `public/test/helpers/TestProvider.tsx` | `Router` from `react-router-dom` | Likely wraps test tree in v5 `<Router>`. Similar pattern. |\n| `public/test/test-utils.tsx` | `Router` from `react-router-dom` | Same — test utility wrapping. |\n\nThe Explore test helper is the most interesting because it uses the v5 `<Route render={props => ...}>` pattern (line 207-221), which is a concrete example of migrating to `<Route element={<Component />}>`.\n\n---\n\n### Tier 3: Structural — the real next step (small but meaningful)\n\nIf you want to test the **actual infrastructure migration** (replacing the v5 `Router` engine), the single file to prototype against is:\n\n| File | What it does |\n|------|-------------|\n| `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx` | The **only** production file that mounts the v5 `<Router history={locationService.getHistory()}>` and `<CompatRouter>`. Replacing this with a v6 `BrowserRouter` or `createBrowserRouter` would be the real milestone — but it also requires `locationService` to stop depending on `history@4.x`. |\n\nThis is the single point of control, so it's small in scope (one file), but large in blast radius (every route flows through it).\n\n---\n\n### Recommended starting order\n\n1. **Start with Tier 1** — the 4 import fixes. Zero risk, and it eliminates all inconsistent import sources from the codebase. You could do all 4 in one PR.\n\n2. **Then tackle Explore's test helper** (`setup.tsx`) as a Tier 2 proof-of-concept. It's the most complex v5 pattern remaining (uses `<Route render={}>` and v5 `<Router>`), and being test-only means you can validate the migration pattern without production risk.\n\n3. **Tier 3** (`RoutesWrapper.tsx`) is the real architectural step — worth spiking on after Tier 1 and 2 are clean, once you have a plan for what `locationService` looks like without `history@4.x`.",
