patterns\n\n---\n\n### 5. \"Add a 'Favorite dashboards' counter to the Browse Dashboards page header\"\n\n**What:** Show a small counter or badge next to the page title on `/dashboards` showing how many dashboards the user has starred.\n\n**Why it's good for a demo:**\n- Touches `BrowseDashboardsPage.tsx`\n- Visible immediately on the browse page\n- Simple API call to get starred count\n- Clean badge/chip visual\n\n---\n\n### 6. \"Add keyboard shortcut hints as tooltips on dashboard toolbar buttons\"\n\n**What:** Wrap the Save, Share, and Settings toolbar buttons with `Tooltip` showing their keyboard shortcuts (e.g., \"Ctrl+S\", \"Ctrl+Shift+S\").\n\n**Why it's good for a demo:**\n- Touches `RightActions.tsx` or individual action button files\n- Visible on hover over any toolbar button\n- Small, self-contained change\n- Feels like a real usability improvement\n\n---\n\n### My recommendation\n\n**Ticket 2 (\"Panel count indicator\")** or **Ticket 3 (\"Copy dashboard UID\")** are the sweet spot:\n\n- **Ticket 2** is the fastest (pure frontend, no API calls, 1 new tiny component + 1 toolbar registration) and produces an always-visible result on any dashboard.\n- **Ticket 3** has slightly more \"wow factor\" because it shows end-to-end interaction (click -> clipboard -> toast notification) and touches the share flow which is a real, meaningful feature surface.\n\nBoth can be completed in under 5 minutes and produce a clear, demonstrable frontend change. You'd create the Jira ticket with a title, description, and acceptance criteria, then use `/do-ticket` to run through the full workflow.",
