  "model": "gpt-5.3-codex",
  "text": "`public/app/features/dashboard-scene/` is Grafana’s **scene-based dashboard runtime**: the place where a dashboard is loaded, represented as a stateful scene graph, rendered, edited, shared, and serialized back to API schema(s).\n\n## What this folder is\n\nThink of it as 4 layers:\n\n- **Route + loading orchestration**: `pages/`  \n  `DashboardScenePage.tsx` mounts the scene and `DashboardScenePageStateManager.ts` fetches data, handles cache, and picks v1/v2 flow.\n- **Core runtime state**: `scene/`  \n  `DashboardScene.tsx` is the central model (title, layout/body, edit mode, overlays, save/discard, panel ops).  \n  `DashboardSceneRenderer.tsx` decides what renders (body, panel edit, settings, overlays).  \n  `DashboardSceneUrlSync.ts` maps URL params (`editview`, `editPanel`, `shareView`, etc.) to scene state.\n- **Persistence boundary**: `serialization/`  \n  `transformSaveModelToScene.ts` maps API/dashboard model -> scene.  \n  `transformSceneToSaveModel.ts` maps scene -> persisted dashboard model.  \n  `DashboardSceneSerializer.ts` abstracts v1 vs v2 serializer behavior.\n- **Feature surfaces**: `settings/`, `sharing/`, `panel-edit/`, `variables/`, `saving/`, `scene/new-toolbar/`  \n  These are domain-specific UI/actions plugged into the core scene.\n\n## How to navigate it effectively\n\nUse this reading order when building a feature:\n\n- Start with `pages/DashboardScenePage.tsx` to see when/where your feature lifecycle starts.\n- Read `pages/DashboardScenePageStateManager.ts` to understand load path, route mode, and v1/v2 selection.\n- Move to `scene/DashboardScene.tsx` to find the authoritative state and action methods you should hook into.\n- Check `scene/DashboardSceneRenderer.tsx` + `scene/DashboardSceneUrlSync.ts` if your feature is URL-driven or overlay/view-driven.\n- Only then dive into your domain folder (`sharing/`, `settings/`, etc.) to implement UI.\n- If feature must persist data, update `serialization/*` and possibly `DashboardSceneSerializer.ts`.\n\n## Practical feature playbook (new feature)\n\n- **New toolbar action**: start in `scene/new-toolbar/` (or `scene/NavToolbarActions.tsx` legacy path), call scene methods on `DashboardScene`.\n- **New settings page**: add a view in `settings/`, then register in `settings/utils.ts` via `createDashboardEditViewFor()` and nav in `useDashboardEditPageNav()`.\n- **New persisted dashboard property**:\n  - Add to `DashboardSceneState` in `scene/DashboardScene.tsx`.\n  - Include in scene->model transform (`serialization/transformSceneToSaveModel.ts` and/or schema-v2 counterpart).\n  - Include in model->scene transform (`serialization/transformSaveModelToScene.ts` and/or schema-v2 counterpart).\n  - Ensure serializer path is correct for v1/v2 (`serialization/DashboardSceneSerializer.ts`).\n- **URL state feature**: add key handling in `scene/DashboardSceneUrlSync.ts`.\n- **Layout/panel behavior**: work under `scene/layout-*` + transform files that convert layout on save/load.\n\n## Mental model that helps avoid getting lost\n\n- `DashboardScene` is the **source of truth**.\n- `Renderer` is just **projection/UI composition**.\n- `UrlSync` is **navigation contract**.\n- `serialization/` is **backend contract**.\n- Everything else is a feature module that manipulates the scene.\n\nIf you want, I can also give you a “feature type -> exact files to touch” checklist for one concrete feature you’re about to build.",
