  "text": "---\n\n## What Was Built\n\nThree files in `~/.cursor/skills/spec-driven-development/`:\n\n| File | Purpose | Lines |\n|------|---------|-------|\n| `SKILL.md` | The skill itself -- process, guardrails, step details, cloud agent optimizations | 168 |\n| `spec-template.md` | The reusable template with all 10 sections, minimum bars, and format examples | 277 |\n| `example-spec.md` | A fully completed spec for an Interactive Kanban Board -- shows what the output looks like | 361 |\n\n## What Makes This Different From Plan Mode\n\n| Plan Mode | This Skill |\n|-----------|------------|\n| General-purpose -- any topic, any format | Purpose-built -- enforced template with 10 required sections |\n| Conversational -- free-flowing discussion | Structured -- sequential steps with quality gates |\n| Output varies by conversation | Output is consistent -- same format every time |\n| No minimum quality bars | Guardrails enforced: 2+ alternatives, all UI states, concrete a11y, numeric perf targets |\n| Requires real-time interaction | Cloud agent optimized -- checkpoints, documented assumptions, self-review |\n\n## How to Run the Demo\n\n**Pre-demo prep** (what you run beforehand):\n\n1. Open a new Cursor chat and say: *\"Using the spec-driven-development skill, write a spec for an interactive kanban board component in React + TypeScript\"*\n2. The agent will follow the 8-step process, produce a structured spec, and hand off to `writing-plans`\n3. From the implementation plan, have the agent build the kanban board\n4. You now have: the skill, the spec it produced, and the working implementation\n\n**During the demo, show the customer:**\n\n1. **The skill file** -- \"Here's our templatized process. 10 required sections, quality gates, guardrails that enforce consistency.\"\n2. **The template** -- \"Every spec follows this exact structure. No section gets skipped, no TBDs left in the final output.\"\n3. **The completed spec** -- \"Here's what it produced. Notice the architecture decision table, the enumerated UI states, the concrete accessibility requirements, the numbered performance targets.\"\n4. **The implementation** -- \"And this is what got built from that spec.\"\n5. **The key message** -- \"Plan mode is powerful. Skills let you take that power and channel it into repeatable, team-specific processes. You can build your own for any workflow -- RFC review, API design, migration planning, whatever your team's process is.\"\n\n## Suggested Refinements\n\nA few things you might want to tweak depending on how the customer's team works:\n\n- **Spec location** -- The skill defaults to `docs/specs/`. If they use a different convention (e.g., RFCs in Notion, ADRs in a specific folder), adjust the save path in Step 8\n- **Template sections** -- If they don't do feature flags, swap section 9 (Rollout Plan) for something they care about (e.g., \"Migration Strategy\" or \"API Contract Review\")\n- **Guardrails strictness** -- The \"minimum 2 non-goals\" and \"minimum 5 edge cases\" bars can be tuned. Some teams want stricter, some looser\n\nWant me to scaffold the actual kanban board React project so you have the full end-to-end demo ready?",
