  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Here's a comprehensive analysis across all three areas.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Frontend Security Vulnerabilities\n\n### Critical\n\n**Scripted dashboards execute arbitrary JavaScript via `new Function`**\n\n`public/app/features/dashboard/services/DashboardLoaderSrv.ts` (lines 71-100) constructs a `new Function(...)` from a loaded dashboard script body and invokes it with `window`, `document`, `$`, and Grafana services as arguments. If dashboard JSON/scripts are not strictly trusted, this is a full same-origin compromise. This is an intentional \"scripted dashboard\" feature, but it's the highest-risk code path in the frontend.\n\n### High\n\n| Issue | Location | Detail |\n|-------|----------|--------|\n| **XSS in trace span table** | `KeyValuesTable.tsx` lines 128-144 | For `row.type === 'code'` and `row.type === 'text'`, `row.value` is interpolated into HTML via `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` **without escaping**. The JSON branch uses `jsonMarkup` which does escape, but the code/text branches do not. Untrusted trace attribute values could execute as HTML/JS. |\n| **`auth_token` in URL query string** | `public/app/core/utils/urlToken.ts` lines 3-12 | Tokens passed via `?auth_token=` are exposed to Referer headers, browser history, server logs, and shoulder-surfing. Also forwarded into WebSocket URLs in `public/app/features/live/centrifuge/service.ts` lines 81-86. |\n\n### Medium\n\n| Issue | Location | Detail |\n|-------|----------|--------|\n| `window.open` without `noopener`/`noreferrer` | `getPanelMenu.ts` ~line 90 | Tabnabbing risk on Ctrl+click panel explore. |\n| Extension-provided link paths | `TracePageHeader.tsx` lines 232-234 | `window.open(link.path, '_blank')` with no validation -- possible `javascript:` URL injection if extensions are untrusted. |\n| Plugin readme raw HTML | `PluginDetailsBody.tsx` lines 56-60 | `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` from `plugin.details?.readme` without `renderMarkdown` sanitization. Trust boundary is \"Grafana.com content\". |\n| `disableSanitizeHtml` config bypass | `link_srv.ts` lines 307, 358 and `TextPanel.tsx` lines 67-90 | Admin-configurable flag that disables HTML sanitization for panels/links. Increases XSS surface if enabled with untrusted content. |\n| Broad `postMessage` target | `app.ts` lines 137-138 | `window.parent.postMessage('GrafanaAppInit', '*')` -- any parent iframe can observe lifecycle events. Low data sensitivity, but violates least-privilege. |\n\n### Low / Informational\n\n- **Console logging in production code**: widespread `console.log`/`console.error` across `backend_srv.ts`, `DashboardScene.tsx`, etc. Risk if logs are shipped to untrusted parties.\n- **Trusted Types**: `public/app/core/trustedTypePolicies.ts` strips `<script` tags and sanitizes URLs. CSP headers are set server-side.\n- **CSRF**: No frontend CSRF token management found; Grafana relies on cookie-based sessions + `SameSite` + server-side middleware.\n- **No prototype pollution patterns** found in `public/app/`.\n\n### Positive security patterns observed\n\n- `renderMarkdown` from `@grafana/data` runs `sanitizeTextPanelContent` by default\n- `textUtil.sanitize` / `sanitizeUrl` used on many hot paths (annotations, news panel, links)\n- Share modal explicitly strips `auth_token` from URLs (`ShareModal/utils.ts` line 56)\n- `loadUrlToken` is aware enough to cache and remove from URL\n\n---\n\n## 2. Testing Framework Overview\n\n### Test Runners\n\n| Runner | Purpose | Config |\n|--------|---------|--------|\n| **Jest** | Unit + integration (primary) | `jest.config.js` (root) -- `jsdom` env, `ts-jest` transform |\n| **Playwright** | E2E (modern) | `playwright.config.ts` -- tests in `e2e-playwright/` |\n| **Cypress** | E2E (legacy) | `cypress.config.js` -- tests in `e2e/` |\n| **Storybook Tests** | Visual/interaction | `playwright.storybook.config.ts` at port 9001 |\n\nNo Vitest is used.\n\n### File Conventions\n\n- **App unit tests**: `*.test.ts` / `*.test.tsx` only (co-located with source)\n- **Package tests**: also allow `*.spec.*` and `__tests__/` directories\n- **E2E tests**: `*.spec.ts` under `e2e-playwright/` (Playwright) and `e2e/` (Cypress)\n\n### Key Testing Utilities\n\n| Utility | Location | Purpose |\n|---------|----------|---------|\n| Custom `render` | `public/test/test-utils.tsx` | Wraps RTL render with Redux `Provider`, `GrafanaContext`, `LocationServiceProvider`, `ModalsContextProvider` |\n| Setup files | `public/test/setupTests.ts`, `jest-setup.ts` | i18n, `jest-fail-on-console`, `@testing-library/jest-dom`, global mocks |\n| Mock factories | `public/test/mocks/` | `getGrafanaContextMock`, `datasource_srv`, workers, etc. |\n| Helpers | `public/test/helpers/` | `createFetchResponse`, `getDashboardModel`, `selectOptionInTest`, `TestProvider` |\n| MSW mocking | `@grafana/test-utils` package | MSW-based HTTP mock utilities for integration tests |\n| Redux testers | `public/test/core/redux/` | `reduxTester.ts`, `reducerTester.ts` |\n\n### Component Testing Stack\n\n**React Testing Library** (`@testing-library/react` + `@testing-library/user-event` + `@testing-library/jest-dom`). No Enzyme. Components should use the custom `render` from `public/test/test-utils.tsx` to get proper Grafana context.\n\n### Coverage\n\n- `yarn test:coverage` runs Jest with `--coverage`\n- Per-codeowner coverage via `jest.config.codeowner.js` using `jest-monocart-coverage` (V8 provider)\n- No global coverage threshold enforced in `jest.config.js`\n- CI workflow `.github/workflows/check-frontend-test-coverage.yml` runs per-team checks for opted-in CODEOWNERS\n\n### CI\n\n- Unit tests: `pr-frontend-unit-tests.yml` with 16-shard matrix\n- E2E: `pr-e2e-tests.yml` (Playwright-based)\n- Scripts: `test:ci` (Jest + `jest-junit` reporter), `e2e:playwright`, `e2e:acceptance`, etc.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Feature Flag Framework\n\n### Architecture Overview\n\n```\nregistry.go (source of truth)\n    │\n    ├── make gen-feature-toggles\n    │       ├── toggles_gen.go (Go constants)\n    │       ├── featureToggles.gen.ts (TypeScript interface)\n    │       ├── toggles_gen.csv / .json (docs)\n    │       └── feature-toggles/index.md (docs)\n    │\n    ├── Backend: FeatureManager (service.go / manager.go)\n    │       └── frontendsettings.go → boot data payload\n    │\n    └── Frontend: config.featureToggles (GrafanaBootConfig)\n            ├── config.featureToggles.<flagName> (legacy)\n            └── evaluateBooleanFlag() (OpenFeature, newer)\n```\n\n### Flag Stages (Lifecycle)\n\nDefined in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/models.go`:\n\n| Stage | Aliases | Meaning |\n|-------|---------|---------|\n| `FeatureStageExperimental` | `alpha` | Internal experiments |\n| `FeatureStagePrivatePreview` | -- | Selected customers |\n| `FeatureStagePublicPreview` | `beta`, `preview` | Broad opt-in |\n| `FeatureStageGeneralAvailability` | `stable`, `ga`, `GA` | Default on |\n| `FeatureStageDeprecated` | `deprecated` | Slated for removal |\n\n### How Flags Are Defined\n\nAll flags live in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go` as `FeatureFlag` structs with `Name`, `Description`, `Stage`, `Expression` (default value), optional `FrontendOnly`, `Owner`, etc. Running `make gen-feature-toggles` regenerates all typed artifacts.\n\n### Frontend Consumption Patterns\n\n**1. Static boot config (legacy, most common):**\n\n```typescript\nimport { config } from '@grafana/runtime';\n\nif (config.featureToggles.myFeature) { ... }\n```\n\n**2. OpenFeature evaluation (newer, recommended for new flags):**\n\n```typescript\nimport { evaluateBooleanFlag } from '@grafana/runtime';\n\nconst isEnabled = evaluateBooleanFlag('myFeature', false);\n```\n\nUses `@openfeature/web-sdk` with `OFREPWebProvider` against the features API.\n\n**3. Concrete usage examples:**\n\n- **Module-level export switching**: `public/app/plugins/panel/gauge/module.tsx` -- `config.featureToggles.newGauge ? pluginV2 : pluginV1`\n- **Conditional routes**: `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` -- gating entire route definitions behind `config.featureToggles.suggestedDashboards`\n- **Runtime evaluation in components**: `BrowseDashboardsPage.tsx` -- `evaluateBooleanFlag('recentlyViewedDashboards', false)`\n- **Non-UI logic branching**: `shortLinks.ts` -- `config.featureToggles.useKubernetesShortURLsAPI`\n- **Scene behavior**: `DashboardScene.tsx` -- multiple flags like `dashboardNewLayouts`, `provisioning`\n\n### Override Mechanisms\n\n| Mechanism | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| INI config | `[feature_toggles]` section: `enable = flag1,flag2` or per-flag `FLAG = true` |\n| Environment vars | `GF_FEATURE_TOGGLES_ENABLE=flag1,flag2` |\n| localStorage | Key `grafana.featureToggles`, comma-separated `name=value` |\n| URL params | `?__feature.myFlag=true` (dev mode allows all; production restricts to \"safe\" flags) |\n\n### Testing with Feature Flags\n\n**Go**: `featuremgmt.WithFeatures(\"flag1\", \"flag2\")` creates a test `FeatureManager`.\n\n**Frontend**: Tests directly mutate `config.featureToggles`:\n\n```typescript\nconfig.featureToggles.myFeature = true;\n// ... test ...\n// restore after\n```\n\nOr mock `evaluateBooleanFlag` via `jest.mocked(evaluateBooleanFlag)`.\n\n### Type Safety\n\nFully type-safe. `FeatureToggles` is a generated TypeScript interface, so `config.featureToggles.nonExistentFlag` is a compile-time error. `evaluateBooleanFlag` accepts `keyof FeatureToggles`. Go side has generated constants in `toggles_gen.go`. Drift is caught by `toggles_gen_test.go` failing if generated files don't match the registry.",
