  "model": "composer-2",
  "text": "\nHere’s a compact **scenario menu** you can pick from, shaped as a three-act demo: **local → pre-baked cloud agent story → live “automation from scratch.”**\n\n---\n\n## Act 1 — Local (5–10 min live)\n\nPurpose: show fast iteration, no cloud dependency, something everyone recognizes.\n\n**Options:**\n\n- **A. “Golden path” bugfix** — small repo change (one file, one test), run tests locally, commit message explains intent.\n- **B. “Spec → tasks”** — you paste a short spec; the agent produces a checklist or Notion/Jira-shaped task list (still local files is fine).\n- **C. “Security / hygiene scan”** — run a linter or a tiny custom script; agent summarizes findings as a markdown report in-repo.\n\nPick one that matches what you’ll actually run on your machine during the demo.\n\n---\n\n## Act 2 — Cloud agents (mostly **pre-run**; you **show** artifacts)\n\nPurpose: show a **bigger, slower, multi-step** job with **visible outputs**: PR(s), design doc, migration plan, test results, screenshots, etc.\n\n**Pre-run ideas (pick one “hero” narrative):**\n\n1. **Cross-cutting refactor**  \n   - Agent: trace a pattern across N packages, propose a migration, open a draft PR with mechanical changes + follow-up TODOs.  \n   - **Artifacts:** diff stats, `MIGRATION.md`, before/after diagram, CI run link.\n\n2. **“Unknown codebase onboarding”**  \n   - Agent: map modules, data flow, and risk areas; produce an architecture note and a “first PR” that adds tests or docs only.  \n   - **Artifacts:** `ARCHITECTURE.md`, dependency sketch, prioritized backlog file.\n\n3. **Feature slice from issue**  \n   - Agent: implement an end-to-end slice (API + UI stub + tests) from a written ticket.  \n   - **Artifacts:** branch/PR, API contract snippet, short Loom-style notes as markdown.\n\n4. **Ops / reliability narrative**  \n   - Agent: add dashboards-as-code, runbooks, or alert rules from a scenario (“latency SLO breach”).  \n   - **Artifacts:** YAML/JSON configs, runbook markdown, “postmortem template” filled partially.\n\n**Demo tip:** On stage you show the **run timeline**, **tool calls summary** (if your product surfaces that), and **open the artifacts**—you don’t need the full hour to elapse live.\n\n---\n\n## Act 3 — Automation from scratch (MCP + webhook **or** schedule)\n\nPurpose: show **repeatable** behavior: triggers, integrations, structured outputs—not a one-off chat.\n\n### Scenario A — **Jira triage + severity** (webhook-first)\n\n- **Trigger:** Jira webhook on issue created/updated (or “comment contains `/triage`”).  \n- **Flow:** Receive payload → fetch issue details via **Jira MCP** (or REST) → classify: component, priority suggestion, duplicate check heuristic, “needs security review” flag → post comment + transition (optional).  \n- **MCP mapping:** Jira (read/write), optional GitHub (link related PRs), optional docs/search MCP for runbooks.  \n- **Why it lands:** Clear input/output on every ticket; easy to show one before/after issue in the UI.\n\n### Scenario B — **PR hygiene / code cleanup bot** (schedule + optional webhook)\n\n- **Trigger:** Nightly schedule **or** GitHub `pull_request` webhook.  \n- **Flow:** List open PRs → for each, run/format/lint suggestions **or** only **report** stale branches, missing tests, TODO debt score → open a summary issue or comment.  \n- **MCP mapping:** GitHub MCP for PR metadata; optional CI API; optional static analysis MCP if you have one.  \n- **Why it lands:** Feels like “automation that pays rent,” not a toy.\n\n### Scenario C — **On-call / severity assistant** (webhook from alerting)\n\n- **Trigger:** Webhook from PagerDuty/Opsgenie/generic JSON (or simulate with `curl`).  \n- **Flow:** Parse alert → pull recent deploys + related dashboards/docs via MCP → produce severity assessment + “first 3 checks” + link block → post to Slack (or write to incident doc).  \n- **MCP mapping:** Datadog/other observability MCP if available; docs/wiki MCP; Slack optional.  \n- **Why it lands:** Strong “real incident” vibe; good if your audience is SRE-heavy.\n\n### Scenario D — **Scheduled “doc drift” / API contract check**\n\n- **Trigger:** Cron weekly.  \n- **Flow:** Compare OpenAPI/spec to implementation hints (or changelog) → MCP reads repo + maybe a schema registry → opens a ticket or PR with “drift report.”  \n- **MCP mapping:** Git + issue tracker; optional API catalog MCP.  \n- **Why it lands:** Clear pass/fail and a PDF/markdown artifact.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested pairings (if you want a default storyline)\n\n| If your audience cares about… | Act 1 local        | Act 2 cloud (pre-run)     | Act 3 automation        |\n|------------------------------|--------------------|---------------------------|-------------------------|\n| **Devs / platform**          | Small bugfix       | Cross-cutting refactor PR | PR hygiene (schedule)   |\n| **PM + eng process**         | Spec → tasks       | Feature slice from issue  | Jira triage (webhook)   |\n| **SRE / ops**                | Lint/scan report   | Runbooks + dashboards     | Alert webhook → triage  |\n\n---\n\n## What to decide next (so you can build artifacts)\n\n1. **Hero for Act 2** — one bullet from the pre-run list (refactor vs onboarding vs feature slice vs ops).  \n2. **Automation spine** — webhook vs schedule (webhook is easier to **trigger live** in a demo; schedule is easier to **explain** as “always on”).  \n3. **Which MCP servers are real for you** — Jira-only vs GitHub-only vs observability stack; the demo is tighter if you only depend on 1–2 integrations you control.\n\nIf you tell me your **audience** (IC engineers vs leadership) and **which MCP servers you actually have wired up**, I can narrow this to **one end-to-end script** (slide titles + exact triggers + artifact checklist) without touching code yet.",
