  "model": "composer-2",
  "text": "\nHere’s a **single, end-to-end scenario** that reads as normal Grafana engineering work in *this* repo, with a clean **local → cloud** split and a **related but separate** automation beat.\n\n---\n\n## Hero scenario: **Serialization round-trip hardening (dashboard scene ↔ save model)**\n\n**Why it feels real:** Every engineer has seen “it works in the UI until you reload” or “export/import dropped a field.” Grafana’s dashboard scene layer leans heavily on transforms like `transformSceneToSaveModel` / `transformSaveModelToScene` (and schema v2 variants). That’s a **durable, on-brand** story without pretending you’re shipping a net-new product surface in one demo.\n\n### Phase 1 — Local (a few prompts: explore → narrow → execute)\n\n**Goal:** Show *tight* iteration: read code, form a hypothesis, land something small and mergeable.\n\nSuggested arc:\n\n1. **Explore** — Pick one vertical slice, e.g. “links,” “tags,” “a specific panel option,” or “meta/preload” — whatever you can trace in one sitting. Skim the transform pair and note “this field is read on load but I don’t see it asserted on save.”\n2. **Execute** — Add a **focused unit test** (or extend an existing one) that fails before a one-line fix, or that locks in current behavior with a clear name so regressions are obvious. Optionally add a tiny **dev-only assertion** or comment in the transform if your story is “document invariant” rather than behavior change.\n\n**Relatable engineer moment:** “I’m not ‘adding a feature’; I’m **stopping silent drift** between scene state and persisted JSON.”\n\n**Concrete artifact for the room:** A PR-sized diff: one test file + minimal production change (or test-only if you prefer zero prod risk for the live local segment).\n\n### Phase 2 — Cloud (plan from local learnings → bigger pre-run)\n\n**Goal:** Same theme, **wider blast radius** — the kind of work that’s boring for a human to grind but great for a cloud agent **you’ve already finished** before the talk.\n\nFrom what you learned locally, the **plan you “send to cloud”** could say things like:\n\n- Inventory transform entrypoints (`transformSceneToSaveModel*.ts`, `transformSaveModelToScene*.ts`, schema v2 paths).\n- Map “persisted props” / `PERSISTED_PROPS`-style lists to **test coverage** (gaps table in `SERIALIZATION_COVERAGE.md` or similar).\n- Add **multiple** tests across panels/settings you didn’t touch locally, or generate a **checklist** of untested fields with owners/suggestions.\n- Optional stretch: a small **script** that greps for `@ts-expect-error` / experimental props and ties them to tests (very Grafana-flavored).\n\n**Artifacts to show on stage:** A longer run summary, a markdown plan, a fatter branch/PR, maybe a table of “field → covered?” — all **finished** before demo day.\n\n**Narrative line:** “Locally I proved the workflow on one gap; in the cloud run I **scaled the same playbook** across the subsystem.”\n\n---\n\n## Automation — related but its **own** demo component\n\nKeep the **same domain** (serialization / dashboard-scene) so the story ties together, but make the automation **procedural** so it’s obviously not “another chat.”\n\n**Automation A — PR webhook (recommended for live trigger)**  \nWhen a PR touches `public/app/features/dashboard-scene/serialization/` (path filter):\n\n1. Pull diff / file list (GitHub API or MCP).\n2. **Rule-based gate:** If any `*.ts` changed under `serialization/` but **no** `*.test.ts` / `*.spec.ts` (or whatever convention you use) changed, post a **short, actionable comment**: “Serialization logic changed; please add/adjust round-trip or golden test (see CONTRIBUTING snippet / example test name).”\n3. Optional: attach a **link** to your local PR or doc from Phase 1 as the “golden example.”\n\n**Automation B — Schedule (nightly / weekly)**  \nCron job that:\n\n- Scans serialization modules for **new exported functions** without a test import, or\n- Runs a lightweight `yarn test` subset and opens/updates a **“serialization health”** issue or Slack message with pass/fail + duration.\n\n**Why this pairs well:** Engineers instantly get “**guardrail on the exact class of mistake** we just fixed manually.” It’s not trying to auto-fix everything; it’s **triage and nudging** — very credible for MCP + webhook/schedule.\n\n---\n\n## If you want a swap-in variant (same structure)\n\n| Variant | Local | Cloud (pre-run) | Automation hook |\n|--------|--------|------------------|-----------------|\n| **“New test harness slice”** | Add one **Playwright** or **RTL** test for a share/settings flow you care about | Broader matrix + flaky-list cleanup doc | Webhook: PRs touching `sharing/` must touch `*.test.tsx` |\n| **“Feature flag retirement”** | Trace one flag, remove one callsite + test | Full removal across packages + changelog | Schedule: report PRs that add `featureToggles.` without tracker ticket in title |\n\n---\n\n## What you’d say in two sentences on stage\n\n- **Local:** “I used a few prompts to **explore the save path** and landed a **real regression test** in the repo everyone recognizes.”  \n- **Cloud:** “I gave the same **structured plan** to a cloud agent and came back to **coverage and docs** I wouldn’t want to produce by hand in an afternoon.”  \n- **Automation:** “Separately, a **webhook** turns that lesson into a **default checklist** for anyone else touching serialization.”\n\nIf you tell me whether you prefer **test-only local change** (zero prod diff) vs **tiny prod fix + test**, I can tighten Phase 1 into a literal prompt sequence (explore → decide → implement) you can paste into your script.",
