  "text": "Here are **two fresh scenarios**. Pick one and I'll finalize the prompt flow, or mix pieces.\n\n---\n\n## Option A — \"Make our error messages actually useful\"\n\nEvery engineer has seen a page that shows a blank screen or a cryptic error when something goes wrong. This is instantly relatable.\n\n**Local:** Find a page that handles errors badly, fix it, test it.  \n**Cloud:** Do the same sweep across the whole feature area.  \n**Automation:** When a PR adds a new API call, check that it handles the error case.\n\n### Prompts\n\n**Local — explore**\n\n1. \"Find me a React component in the dashboard feature that makes an API call but doesn't show a useful error if it fails.\"\n\n2. \"Show me what happens right now when that call fails — what does the user see?\"\n\n**Local — fix**\n\n3. \"Fix it. Show a clear error message instead and let the user retry.\"\n\n4. \"Add a test that checks the error state renders correctly.\"\n\n5. \"Run the tests for this file.\"\n\n**Cloud — handoff**\n\n6. \"Write me a plan to do this same thing across the rest of the dashboard feature — find every API call with bad or missing error handling, list the files, and describe what each one needs.\"\n\n*Then on stage:* \"I sent that plan to a cloud agent yesterday — here's the PR it produced.\"\n\n**Automation**\n\n7. \"I want to set up an automation: when a PR is opened that adds a new fetch or API call, check whether there's error handling around it. If not, leave a comment asking for it.\"\n\n8. \"Use GitHub as the trigger and keep the logic simple — just pattern matching on the diff, not full static analysis.\"\n\n---\n\n## Option B — \"Onboard to unfamiliar code, then clean it up\"\n\nThis is the most universal engineering task: you land in code you didn't write, you need to understand it, and you leave it better than you found it.\n\n**Local:** Pick a feature folder you've never opened, get oriented, find something messy, clean one thing up.  \n**Cloud:** Do a full cleanup + documentation pass on that feature area.  \n**Automation:** Flag new PRs that introduce common code smells in that area.\n\n### Prompts\n\n**Local — explore**\n\n1. \"I've never looked at the provisioning feature in this repo. Give me a quick tour — what are the main files, what do they do, how do they connect?\"\n\n2. \"What's the messiest part? Something that's too long, does too many things, or is hard to follow.\"\n\n**Local — fix**\n\n3. \"Pick the easiest win and refactor it — break out a function, rename something confusing, whatever makes the biggest difference with the smallest change.\"\n\n4. \"Make sure nothing broke.\"\n\n**Cloud — handoff**\n\n5. \"Write a plan to clean up the rest of this feature area. Include what you'd rename, split, or restructure, and what order to do it in.\"\n\n*Then on stage:* \"I gave this plan to a cloud agent — here's what it came back with.\"\n\n**Automation**\n\n6. \"Set up an automation: once a week, scan this feature area for files over 300 lines, functions over 50 lines, and any TODOs older than 6 months. Post a summary to a GitHub issue.\"\n\n7. \"Also add a PR trigger: if someone adds a new file to provisioning without a corresponding test file, leave a comment.\"\n\n---\n\n## Which one?\n\n- **Option A** (error handling) is better if your audience is **product-minded** — the before/after is visible and the \"why\" is obvious.\n- **Option B** (onboard + cleanup) is better if your audience is **engineering-heavy** — \"I inherited this code\" is the most relatable sentence in software.\n\nTell me which one (or a mix) and I'll finalize the prompt list ready to go.",
